import med, lohi as L
med.GENERIC.update({
"relax":"It acts mainly in the brain and spinal cord to dampen nerve signals that keep muscles tense, and is cleared mostly by the liver.",
"barb":"Like other barbiturates, it holds GABA-A chloride channels open longer and, at higher levels, opens them directly, which is why barbiturates can shut down breathing on their own.",
"hyp":"It acts on the brain's sleep and arousal systems and is cleared mostly by the liver.",
"gaba":"It binds the alpha-2-delta subunit of calcium channels on nerve cells, reducing the release of excitatory signals; it is cleared unchanged by the kidneys."})
E=lambda o1,o2,p1,p2,c1,c2:{"onset":[o1,o2],"peak":[p1,p2],"comedown":[c1,c2]}
med.EXTRA.update({
"relax":E("Medically, the onset reflects absorption from the gut and entry into the brain. {met} Muscle relaxants work mostly through central sedation rather than acting on the muscles themselves (dantrolene is the exception), so drowsiness usually arrives together with the loosening of tension.","Clinicians warn that reaction time slows early, often before the person notices, and that dizziness on standing is common, especially in older adults.",
"Medically, the peak is when sedation and dizziness are strongest. Several muscle relaxants have anticholinergic or blood-pressure effects as well, which can cause dry mouth, blurred vision, a fast or slow heartbeat, or fainting.","Alone they rarely stop breathing in healthy adults, but combined with opioids, benzodiazepines, alcohol, or gabapentinoids they add to respiratory depression; carisoprodol in particular was a common part of the deadly 'holy trinity' with opioids and benzodiazepines.",
"As levels fall, sedation lifts and muscle tension may return. Short-acting drugs wear off within hours, while drugs with active metabolites (carisoprodol to meprobamate) linger.","People who use carisoprodol, meprobamate, or baclofen daily can develop dependence; stopping suddenly can cause anxiety, insomnia, hallucinations, and seizures, and abrupt baclofen withdrawal can be life-threatening."),
"barb":E("Medically, the onset reflects how quickly the barbiturate dissolves in fat and crosses into the brain. {met} Short-acting barbiturates enter the brain fast; long-acting phenobarbital is slower.","Early signs are like alcohol intoxication: loosened inhibitions, slurred speech, unsteady walking, and slowed thinking. Judgment fades early, which is part of why accidental overdose was so common in the 20th century.",
"Medically, the peak is when GABA-A channels are held open longest. Unlike benzodiazepines, barbiturates at higher levels can open these channels directly, so there is no ceiling: breathing, blood pressure, and body temperature can all fall, and coma can follow.","The gap between sedation and fatal poisoning is narrow, and alcohol greatly narrows it further. There is no antidote like naloxone; treatment is breathing support in hospital.",
"As levels fall, the person may remain clumsy and confused for hours, and barbiturates disturb normal sleep stages, so people often wake unrefreshed.","Tolerance to the pleasant effects builds faster than tolerance to the breathing effects, so the danger stays while the high fades. Withdrawal after daily use is among the most dangerous of any drug, with seizures, delirium, and high fever; it needs medical care."),
"hyp":E("Medically, the onset reflects absorption and entry into the brain. {met} Most hypnotics are designed to work quickly so the person falls asleep soon after taking them.","Clinicians advise taking hypnotics only right before bed, because people who stay awake can experience confusion, hallucinations, and memory loss.",
"Medically, the peak is when sleep pressure is strongest. People who resist sleep at this point may walk, eat, talk, drive, or send messages with no memory afterward ('complex sleep behaviors'), which led to a 2019 FDA boxed warning for zolpidem, zaleplon, and eszopiclone.","Combined with alcohol, opioids, or other sedatives, breathing can slow dangerously, and amnesia is much more likely.",
"As levels fall, most people sleep through the comedown. Those who wake early can feel groggy, unsteady, and slow, and next-morning impairment can affect driving, especially in women and older adults.","Nightly use can cause tolerance and rebound insomnia on stopping; some hypnotics (GHB, methaqualone, older drugs) cause severe dependence and dangerous withdrawal."),
"gaba":E("Medically, the onset reflects slow and variable absorption. {met} Gabapentin is absorbed by a transporter in the gut that saturates, so more is not absorbed in proportion; pregabalin is absorbed more fully and quickly.","Early effects include calm, mild dizziness, and relaxation. Because gabapentinoids are cleared by the kidneys, people with kidney disease or older adults are affected more strongly and for longer.",
"Medically, the peak brings the strongest dizziness, unsteadiness, and sedation. Some people feel calm sociability or mild euphoria; others feel only clumsy and sleepy.","In 2019 the FDA warned that gabapentinoids can cause serious breathing problems when combined with opioids or other depressants, or in people with lung disease or older age. Gabapentinoids appear in many opioid overdose deaths.",
"As levels fall, dizziness and sedation fade over several hours. Sleep is often deep.","Daily use can cause dependence; stopping suddenly can cause anxiety, insomnia, sweating, pain, agitation, and occasionally seizures, so medical tapering is advised."),
})
med.VARY.update({k:"Why the same amount affects people differently: tolerance, body size, age, liver and kidney function, genetics, and especially other depressants (alcohol, opioids, benzodiazepines, gabapentinoids, antihistamines) change the effect. Older adults and people with sleep apnea or lung disease are at higher risk. Stronger effects do not stop at more pleasure: they shade into blackouts, falls, and slowed breathing." for k in("relax","barb","hyp","gaba")})
L.CLS.update({
"relax":"At lower doses, muscle relaxants mainly cause drowsiness and eased tension. At higher doses, dizziness, heavy sedation, poor coordination, blurred vision, and confusion grow, and some (carisoprodol, meprobamate, orphenadrine, baclofen, tizanidine) bring additional dangers: seizures, heart rhythm problems, very low blood pressure, or coma. With opioids, alcohol, or benzodiazepines, breathing can slow and stop.",
"barb":"At lower doses, barbiturates cause calm, loosened inhibitions, and a drunk-like feeling. At higher doses, slurred speech, staggering, confusion, and deep sleep follow. As effects keep rising, the person cannot be woken, breathing and blood pressure fall, and body temperature drops: barbiturates can be fatal on their own, and far more easily with alcohol.",
"hyp":"At lower doses, hypnotics bring sleepiness and calm. At higher doses, or when the person stays awake, confusion, memory loss, hallucinations, and sleep-walking or sleep-driving become likely. With alcohol or opioids, or with drugs like GHB and methaqualone, effects can tip into unconsciousness, vomiting while unconscious, and stopped breathing.",
"gaba":"At lower doses, gabapentinoids cause calm, mild dizziness, and relaxation. At higher doses, unsteadiness, slurred speech, double vision, and heavy sedation increase, and some people feel a drunk-like euphoria. With opioids or other depressants, breathing can slow dangerously."})
DROUTE={"oral":"Swallowing is the standard medical route. Effects build gradually; food can slow onset.","injected":"Injection is a hospital route for anesthesia, seizures, or emergencies, with monitoring of breathing and blood pressure.","rectal":"Some sedatives have rectal forms used in hospitals or for children.","intranasal":"Not a standard medical route for most of these drugs; snorting pills damages the nose and makes effects faster and less predictable.","sublingual-buccal":"Under-the-tongue forms exist for a few sleep medicines and act a little faster than swallowed tablets.","inhaled-smoked":"Not a medical route.","transdermal":"Not a medical route."}
DRISK={"oral":"Slow onset tempts people to take more before the first amount peaks. Long-acting drugs build up over days. Mixing with alcohol, opioids, or benzodiazepines is the main cause of deaths.","injected":"A hospital route. Breathing and blood pressure can drop quickly. Injecting pills causes vein damage, infection, and blood clots.","rectal":"Sedation and slowed breathing must be watched afterward.","intranasal":"Nasal damage and faster, less predictable sedation.","sublingual-buccal":"Slightly faster onset, so take it only in bed.","inhaled-smoked":"Not a typical route; lung irritation and unpredictable effects.","transdermal":"Not a medical route."}
